# GAVO Data Center

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:space_tap, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19.1"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1.13"}
])
```

## The service

The [GAVO Data Center](https://dc.g-vo.org) in Heidelberg publishes hundreds of tables, from historical catalogues to the IVOA Registry itself. It runs [DaCHS](https://docs.g-vo.org/DaCHS/), the software behind many TAP services, so it is a good second implementation to try SpaceTAP against.

This notebook looks at what the service holds, draws the naked-eye sky from the Hipparcos catalogue, searches the Registry for other TAP services, and ends with the errors DaCHS sends.

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

gavo = "https://dc.g-vo.org/tap"

to_maps = fn %SpaceTAP.Result{columns: columns, rows: rows} ->
  Enum.map(rows, fn row -> columns |> Enum.zip(row) |> Map.new() end)
end

table = fn result, name ->
  Kino.DataTable.new(to_maps.(result), keys: result.columns, name: name)
end
```

## What the service holds

Every TAP service describes its own tables and columns in `TAP_SCHEMA`, which you query with ADQL like any other table (TAP 1.1 §4). The schemas with the most tables:

```elixir
{:ok, schemas} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, """
  SELECT TOP 15 schema_name, COUNT(*) AS n_tables
  FROM tap_schema.tables
  GROUP BY schema_name
  ORDER BY n_tables DESC
  """)

table.(schemas, "Schemas")
```

The columns of a table, with their units, come from `tap_schema.columns`. Here, the Hipparcos catalogue:

```elixir
{:ok, columns} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, """
  SELECT column_name, unit, description
  FROM tap_schema.columns
  WHERE table_name = 'hipparcos.main'
  """)

table.(columns, "hipparcos.main columns")
```

Look at the units before using a column: here `parallax` is in degrees, not in the milliarcseconds most catalogues use.

## The naked-eye sky

Hipparcos, an ESA satellite and Gaia's predecessor, measured about 118,000 stars. Those brighter than magnitude 4 are roughly the stars you can see from a city:

```elixir
{:ok, bright} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, """
  SELECT hipno, ra, dec, vmag, sptype AS spectral_type
  FROM hipparcos.main
  WHERE vmag < 4
  ORDER BY vmag
  """)

length(bright.rows)
```

`sptype AS spectral_type` names the column in the result. Without the alias, DaCHS writes the header as `SpType`: ADQL names that aren't in double quotes ignore case (ADQL 2.1 §2.1.7), so the service may spell them its own way.

The first letter of the spectral type is the spectral class, from hot blue O stars to cool red M stars, so colour the dots by it:

```elixir
classes = ~w(O B A F G K M)

stars =
  for star <- to_maps.(bright) do
    {vmag, ""} = Float.parse(star["vmag"])
    {ra, ""} = Float.parse(star["ra"])
    {dec, ""} = Float.parse(star["dec"])
    class = String.first(star["spectral_type"] || "")

    %{
      "hip" => star["hipno"],
      "ra" => ra,
      "dec" => dec,
      "vmag" => vmag,
      "brightness" => 4.5 - vmag,
      "class" => if(class in classes, do: class, else: "other")
    }
  end

Vl.new(width: 700, height: 350, title: "Stars brighter than magnitude 4")
|> Vl.data_from_values(stars)
|> Vl.mark(:circle, opacity: 0.8)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "ra", type: :quantitative, title: "RA (°)", scale: [domain: [360, 0]])
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "dec", type: :quantitative, title: "Dec (°)", scale: [domain: [-90, 90]])
|> Vl.encode_field(:size, "brightness",
  type: :quantitative,
  legend: nil,
  scale: [range: [5, 250]]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "class",
  type: :nominal,
  title: "Class",
  scale: [
    domain: classes ++ ["other"],
    range: ~w(#5b7cfa #8fb0ff #d6e2ff #fff4d6 #ffe08a #ffb35c #ff7a45 #999999)
  ]
)
|> Vl.encode(:tooltip, [
  [field: "hip", type: :nominal, title: "HIP"],
  [field: "vmag", type: :quantitative, title: "V"],
  [field: "class", type: :nominal]
])
```

Hover over a dot to see its HIP number and magnitude. The biggest, at RA 101° and Dec −17°, is Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky.

## The nearest stars

Sort by parallax to get the nearest stars. With the parallax in degrees, multiply by 3,600,000 to get milliarcseconds; the distance in parsecs is 1000 divided by that, and a parsec is 3.26 light years:

```elixir
{:ok, nearest} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, """
  SELECT TOP 10 hipno, vmag, parallax, sptype AS spectral_type
  FROM hipparcos.main
  ORDER BY parallax DESC
  """)

nearest_stars =
  for star <- to_maps.(nearest) do
    {parallax_deg, ""} = Float.parse(star["parallax"])
    parallax_mas = parallax_deg * 3_600_000
    distance_pc = 1000 / parallax_mas

    %{
      "hip" => star["hipno"],
      "vmag" => star["vmag"],
      "spectral_type" => star["spectral_type"],
      "parallax_mas" => Float.round(parallax_mas, 1),
      "distance_ly" => Float.round(distance_pc * 3.2616, 2)
    }
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(nearest_stars,
  keys: ~w(hip vmag spectral_type parallax_mas distance_ly),
  name: "Nearest Hipparcos stars"
)
```

HIP 70890 is Proxima Centauri, and the next two are Alpha Centauri A and B. Proxima is faint (V = 11) although it is the nearest: it is a small red dwarf.

## Searching the Registry for TAP services

The IVOA Registry lists the services of the Virtual Observatory. GAVO serves it through TAP too, with the tables defined by [RegTAP](https://www.ivoa.net/documents/RegTAP/), so you can search it with ADQL. `ivo_hasword` is a RegTAP function for word searches in free text. TAP services whose description mentions a word:

```elixir
word = "Gaia"

{:ok, services} =
  SpaceTAP.query(gavo, """
  SELECT res.res_title, intf.access_url
  FROM rr.resource AS res
  JOIN rr.capability AS cap ON cap.ivoid = res.ivoid
  JOIN rr.interface AS intf ON intf.ivoid = cap.ivoid AND intf.cap_index = cap.cap_index
  WHERE cap.standard_id = 'ivo://ivoa.net/std/tap'
    AND intf.intf_role = 'std'
    AND 1 = ivo_hasword(res.res_description, '#{word}')
  ORDER BY res.res_title
  """)

table.(services, "TAP services that mention #{word}")
```

`access_url` is the service URL to pass to `SpaceTAP.query/3`. Many are registered with `http://`: if the service also answers on `https://`, use that, because a redirect from `http://` to `https://` loses the query (see the README).

Interpolating `word` into the ADQL is fine for a word you typed yourself. ADQL has no bound parameters, so never interpolate text from someone else without escaping it.

## Errors from DaCHS

DaCHS answers invalid ADQL with HTTP 500 and a VOTable error document. The document starts with an `xml-stylesheet` processing instruction, so a browser can show it as a web page:

```elixir
SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELEC TOP 3 hipno FROM hipparcos.main")
```

An unknown column:

```elixir
SpaceTAP.query(gavo, "SELECT TOP 3 b_v FROM hipparcos.main")
```

Both are `:service_error`s with the message DaCHS wrote: the document says its query status is `ERROR` (DALI 1.1 §4.4), so the HTTP status doesn't decide the type.
